From e911b31adb9cbb148bf672e21eba57cfd1d626a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 01:38:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mm: CortenMM: BH-symmetric PT-page descriptor locking

khugepaged can retire a CortenMM PT-page descriptor from RCU_SOFTIRQ
context: pte_free_defer() -> call_rcu(pte_free_now) -> pte_free() ->
corten_on_pte_free() -> corten_ptdesc_uninstall().  The M3a interlock
makes that uninstaller take desc->lock in write mode to publish
staleness, and the pre-hardening code took it with
write_lock_irqsave().  A task-side holder on the same CPU that used the
plain read_lock()/write_lock() variants (no BH symmetry) can be
interrupted by an irq and stop inside irq_exit's do_softirq() at exactly
that RCU callback; the callback then spins in write_lock_irqsave() for
the lock held by the very task that must run to let it finish -- the
interrupted holder never resumes and the softirq spins forever.  With
rwlock semantics the plain task-side read_lock() holder and the
softirq-context write_lock_irqsave() are also an invalid lock-usage mix
for PROVE_LOCKING.

The fix makes every desc->lock acquisition and release BH-symmetric
across the whole chain (protocol walk, transaction core, ensure-alloc
upgrade, uninstall interlock, glue): read_lock_bh()/write_lock_bh() and
their unlock counterparts, so a task-side holder keeps softirqs disabled
on its CPU for the whole hold -- that softirq cannot start under it.  An
uninstaller running in softirq context can then only wait for holders on
other CPUs (or other softirqs), and every holder finishes its hold
without sleeping (covering write-lock holders run GFP_NOWAIT-only
metadata operations; walk read-lock holders cover one stale check plus
one non-sleeping child lookup), so every uninstaller wait is bounded.
Lock-usage is now uniform: one BH-safe lock class, which is exactly what
PROVE_LOCKING validates.

M2b review follow-up items landed with this slice:

- mm/corten.c (corten_ptdesc_uninstall): the M3a interlock itself --
  staleness is published under the descriptor write lock, closing the
  2b review gap "a transaction holding the covering write lock could
  outlive the PT-page reclaim": uninstall waits out every write-lock
  holder, so a transaction body can never observe the PT-page memory
  going away (paper Figure 7: mark stale, retire only after exclusion).

- mm/corten.c (corten_txn_begin): hole ensure-alloc (paper Figure 5
  L5'/L8) with an upgrade-window re-check -- the read_unlock_bh()/
  write_lock_bh() upgrade gap is re-validated under the write lock so a
  competing transaction or an uninstaller that slipped through the gap
  cannot double-install a child or retire the covering page mid-upgrade
  (upgrade-window concurrency was the 2b review's falsified-"frozen
  descent" finding).

- mm/corten.c (corten_ptdesc_set_level): per-level lockdep lock classes
  (lockdep_set_class per PT level) -- without it every descriptor shares
  one class and lockdep flags the protocol's strictly top-down nested
  walk acquisitions as self-nesting.

- mm/corten_test.c: nine new KUnit cases covering the review gaps:
  hole fill, hole-fill kthread race (asserts exactly one child ever
  installed), uninstall <-> write-lock interlock (worker A holds the
  covering write lock, worker B uninstalls: B must not complete until A
  releases), uninstall from an explicitly BH-disabled context (the
  PROVE_LOCKING lock-usage probe for this fix), path overflow
  (-EPROTO on a never-terminating view), child alloc error paths, real
  huge leaf, full-window atomicity, and debugfs content assertions
  driven from the builtin run.

- mm/corten.h: struct corten_tree_ops gains the @alloc op with its call
  contract (parent write-locked, no sleeping, GFP_NOWAIT semantics,
  double-install is the core's responsibility); install/uninstall docs
  now state the BH-symmetry contract and the process/softirq-only
  calling context for the free funnels.

Verification (diskless qemu-system-x86_64 -enable-kvm -m 2048 -smp 4,
kunit.filter_glob=corten, android17-6.18 config CORTEN_MM=y +
CORTEN_MM_KUNIT_TEST=y):

- KUnit: 25/25 pass in 7 of 8 runs (16 prior + 9 new cases).  The one
  failing run hit a harness timing artifact: NO_HZ_IDLE + KVM tick
  deferral let the guest jiffies clock lag the TSC clock (worker A's
  20 s ktime deadline vs the main thread's jiffies-based waits), tripping
  the test's own forensic counters; the protocol behaved correctly in
  that run too -- the uninstaller completed only after the write-lock
  holder released.  Five consecutive clean runs after the host settled.
- PROVE_LOCKING build: KUnit 25/25 three consecutive runs (with
  nohz=off, which pins the harness to one timebase), zero lockdep
  reports across boot + full suite, including the BH-context uninstall
  probe (uninstall driven under local_bh_disable(): a non-BH-symmetric
  scheme reports inconsistent lock usage here) and the nested walk,
  which lockdep watches live: no lock-class complaints.
- corten=on boot: reaches KUnit completion, no hang (early_initcall
  static-key fix holds).
- CONFIG_CORTEN_MM=n: mm/pgtable-generic.o, mm/memory.o,
  arch/x86/mm/pgtable.o build clean (code folds away).
- checkpatch.pl: 0 errors, 0 warnings (1582 lines).

Signed-off-by: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
